<Project>

  <!-- Shared properties for every project in the solution. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    NuGet Audit: a package with a known high or critical vulnerability fails the restore, wherever
    it sits in the dependency graph.

    Dependabot's version updates see only the packages a project references directly. NuGet Audit
    checks every package in the restored graph, transitive ones included, against nuget.org's
    vulnerability data (the GitHub Advisory Database) on every restore. By default it only warns,
    and a warning in the Restore step does not fail CI.

    - NuGetAuditMode=all. The .NET 10 SDK already defaults to all, but only for projects that
      target net10.0 or later (NuGet.targets in the SDK); a project targeting an older framework
      would silently fall back to direct. Stated here so that it cannot.
    - NuGetAuditLevel stays at its default, low: low and moderate advisories are still reported,
      as warnings.
    - NU1903 (high) and NU1904 (critical) are errors. NU1900, the vulnerability data could not be
      fetched, stays a warning, so that a transient fault on nuget.org does not block merging by
      itself. It is a gap while it lasts, visible in the Restore step's log.

    The consequence is intended (fail closed): an advisory published against a package that is
    already in the graph fails the next restore with no change to the repository. Until that
    package is updated, `Format, build and test` fails on every pull request and on main, so
    nothing merges, and the restore in deploy.yml fails, so nothing deploys. Local builds fail
    too. The fix is to update the package; for a transitive one whose parent has no fixed
    release yet, a direct reference to the fixed version lifts it (as IIoT.Functions does for
    Azure.Messaging.EventHubs). Only where no fixed version exists, a NuGetAuditSuppress item
    with the advisory URL and the reason is the documented, reviewable exception.

    Not covered: the Functions extensions project that Azure.Functions.Sdk generates under
    src/IIoT.Functions/obj/azure_functions/. The SDK sets ImportDirectoryBuildProps=false and
    net8.0 for it, so it never reads this file and is audited with NuGet's defaults for net8.0:
    its direct package only, and as a warning
    (https://github.com/asnielsen789/IIoT-platform/issues/35).

    https://learn.microsoft.com/nuget/concepts/auditing-packages
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1903;NU1904</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
